# Context compression (optional)

An *optional* layer you can add on top of any agent in this project. The
template does **not** ship it, configure it, or wire it in — this page explains
what it is, when it helps, and the risks to weigh before installing it.

For the built-in context discipline the template already prescribes, see
[Working with agents](working-with-agents.md) (Context hygiene, Token economy).

## What it is

Context compression tools sit between the agent and the LLM provider. They
rewrite tool outputs, logs, file reads, and conversation history so the prompt
contains fewer tokens *before* it is sent. The leading example is
[Headroom](https://github.com/headroomlabs-ai/headroom) (Apache-2.0, local-first):
a proxy / library / MCP server with content-aware compressors (AST for code,
structural for JSON, an ML model for prose), a cache-aligner that keeps provider
KV-caches hitting, and a reversible store (CCR) so the agent can retrieve the
originals on demand.

## Why the template does not ship it

The template's design principles (Pareto, KISS, Security by default, SPOT)
govern this call:

- **Pareto over completeness.** Compression is a power-user optimisation, not a
  need of the 80 % of fresh Python projects the template targets.
- **KISS.** A proxy plus a CLI plus an ML model download plus extra env vars is
  real cognitive and install cost — the wrong default for a one-command scaffold.
- **Security by default.** A compression proxy sees *every* API key and *every*
  prompt the agent sends. That is a new credential surface, and the principle
  requires a deny-rule / guard review *before* the surface ships — not something
  a generated project should inherit by default.
- **SPOT.** Some compressors (`headroom learn`) write corrections directly into
  `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md`. Behaviour rules in this template live exclusively
  in `AGENTS.md` and `guidelines/*.md`; an outside tool rewriting them violates
  the single-source-of-truth rule and the "don't edit your own guardrails" rule
  in `AGENTS.md`.

So: opt-in for the developer who wants it, never the default.

## When it is worth adding

- You run AI coding agents daily and your token bill is the limiting factor.
- You work across multiple agents (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Aider) and
  would benefit from a shared cross-agent memory store.
- You need *reversible* compression — originals retrievable on demand — rather
  than lossy summary.
- You are on a metered backend (LiteLLM gateway, per-token API) where 47–92 %
  fewer input tokens directly lowers cost.

## When to skip it

- You are on a flat-rate subscription (Claude Max, ChatGPT Plus) where tokens
  are not the constraint.
- You already use the provider's native compaction and don't need cross-agent
  memory.
- You are in a sandboxed environment where a local proxy cannot run.

## Risks to weigh before installing

- **Credential surface.** The proxy terminates TLS to the provider and sees
  every key and every prompt. Run it locally; never point it at shared keys in
  CI without scoping. Treat the proxy process like any other credential-bearing
  tool.
- **Guardrail edits.** `headroom learn --target AGENTS.md` (and the equivalent
  for `CLAUDE.md` / `GEMINI.md`) writes to the project's rule files. Prefer the
  default `CLAUDE.local.md` target (git-ignored, personal) and **do not** point
  it at the shared `AGENTS.md` — that would silently rewrite the rules every
  other agent on the project follows.
- **Tool-config collisions.** `headroom wrap <tool>` injects its own config into
  the very files the template already configures (`.claude/settings.json`,
  `opencode.json`, `.aider.conf.yml`, `.codex/config.toml`). Two config sources
  for the same tool fight each other; reconcile by hand after wrapping.
- **Install surface.** The Python build backend pulls `rustup` and an ONNX
  runtime; the ML compressor pulls a model from Hugging Face. In corporate /
  SSL-inspecting networks this needs `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` / `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`
  or a pre-provisioned runtime. Not a blocker, but a friction point to know
  about.

## How to install it yourself

This is a sketch, not a template-managed path — verify against the upstream
docs, which change frequently.

```bash
# 1 — install (Python CLI; the npm package is SDK-only, no CLI)
pipx install --python python3.13 "headroom-ai[all]"

# 2 — wrap the agent you use (one at a time)
headroom wrap claude          # or: codex | opencode | aider | ...

# 3 — check it is routing
headroom doctor
headroom dashboard           # live savings, proxy must be running
```

Undo durable wrapping with `headroom unwrap <tool>`. The proxy must be running
for compression to take effect; `headroom wrap` starts it for you.

After wrapping, re-open the tool config file the template generated
(`.claude/settings.json`, `opencode.json`, …) and make sure Headroom's injected
entries do not duplicate or contradict the template's own backend wiring. When
in doubt, keep the template's backend URL and model alias; let Headroom own only
the compression surface.

## Alternatives

- **Native compaction** — Claude Code `/compact`, Codex `/summarise`. Free,
  built in, lossy. Good enough for many sessions; no extra install.
- **RTK** / **lean-ctx** — CLI wrappers that rewrite shell-output specifically
  (`git show --short`, scoped `ls`, summarised installers). Narrower scope than
  Headroom, lighter weight, not reversible.
- **Provider compaction** — OpenAI's conversation-history compaction. Provider-
  native, not portable across agents.

## Rule of thumb

If you are asking "should I add a compression proxy?", first check whether you
have actually hit the limits the built-in discipline already covers: compact at
~70 %, clear on topic switch, lazy-load source, cache-friendly system blocks.
Most projects never need more than that. Reach for a compression tool when the
token bill says you do — not because the README has impressive percentages.

